= Wave 0 compiled context =

'''State: Phase 2 — Context compilation + writing.'''

This page normalizes the Wave 0 capture into shared definitions, boundaries, naming, depth behavior, and link targets before article prose is rewritten.

== Depth surface convention ==

For a canonical concept <code>Title</code>:

* <code>Title</code> — '''Core''': normal BITwiki article, roughly Wikipedia/Scholarpedia depth.
* <code>Title/Simple</code> — '''Simple''': plain-language orientation with low prerequisite load.
* <code>Title/Technical</code> — '''Technical''': formalism, implementation, edge cases, standards, and advanced distinctions.

All three surfaces describe the same concept. They are not three independent ontological objects.

Frontier/research material is not a fourth reading level. It belongs in explicit sections such as <code>Open questions</code>, <code>Current research</code>, or <code>Controversies</code> on Core/Technical pages.

== Linking rule ==

Reader-facing links should be contextual and dense, like a well-linked MediaWiki encyclopedia.

* Core pages link to canonical Core titles.
* Simple pages should prefer Simple variants where they exist, otherwise Core.
* Technical pages should prefer Technical variants where they exist, otherwise Core.
* Piped links are encouraged when normal prose needs a different grammatical form.
* SMW relationships are used only for stable machine-readable relations; ordinary contextual links do not need an SMW property.

== Normalized Wave 0 definitions ==

=== Algorithm ===
A well-specified effective procedure for transforming input/state toward a result under a stated computational model. Classical definitions usually require finite description and termination for a completed result; modern usage also includes randomized, interactive, online, and long-running procedures, so claims about termination must state the intended model.

=== Computation ===
The carrying out of rule-governed state transitions under a computational model. Abstract computation studies formal models independently of physical realization; concrete computation concerns physical systems implementing such models.

=== Data structure ===
An organization and representation of data together with relations/operations relevant to storage and computation. Distinguish the concrete/representational data structure from an abstract data type, which specifies behavior independently of representation.

=== Program (computing) ===
An artifact or specification in an executable/interpretable representation that directs computational behavior. A program is not an executing process, and is not identical to either an algorithm or an entire software system.

=== Programming language ===
A notation and rule system for expressing programs/computations, characterized by syntax and semantics and typically accompanied by rules for names, types, abstraction, control, state, and interaction. A language is distinct from its implementations.

=== Software ===
Programs plus associated computational artifacts and information treated as part of an operational software system in a stated context. The exact boundary can include configuration, libraries, data, documentation, models, or deployment artifacts depending on the engineering purpose.

=== Version control ===
A system/practice for recording, identifying, comparing, retrieving, branching, merging, and attributing revisions of versioned artifacts over time. It is a component of broader configuration management, not a substitute for it.

=== System boundary ===
A purpose- and observer-dependent distinction separating a system of interest from its environment for analysis, design, explanation, or intervention. A system boundary can be physical, organizational, informational, temporal, or conceptual.

=== Hypothesis ===
A proposed explanatory or testable claim used in inquiry. A hypothesis is distinct from a prediction, and the general scientific concept must be distinguished from the narrower statistical use of null/alternative hypotheses.

=== Causality ===
The family of relations and formalisms in which one event, variable, condition, process, or intervention makes a difference to another. No single metaphysical theory is universally accepted; Core coverage must preserve regularity, counterfactual, probabilistic, mechanistic, interventionist, structural-causal, and potential-outcomes perspectives without treating them as interchangeable.

== Shared non-equivalences ==

<pre>
algorithm ≠ program ≠ computation
program ≠ process
program ⊂/participates in software, but program ≠ software
programming language ≠ compiler/interpreter/runtime
data structure ≠ abstract data type
hypothesis ≠ prediction ≠ theory
scientific hypothesis ≠ statistical hypothesis
causality ≠ correlation
system boundary ≠ interface ≠ necessarily physical edge
version control ≠ backup ≠ full configuration management
</pre>

